Load VertexIndex before BaseVertex, and InstanceIndex before BaseInstance, in source order.

The SPIR-V legalization of SV_VertexID and SV_InstanceID (source/slang/slang-ir-glsl-legalize.cpp)
replaces each load with VertexIndex - BaseVertex (InstanceIndex - BaseInstance), writing the two
loads as the arguments of one call: `builder.emitSub(type, builder.emitLoad(vertexIndex),
builder.emitLoad(baseVertex))`. C++ leaves the order in which those arguments are evaluated
unspecified, and host compilers differ: the x86-64 and aarch64 builds of Slang emit the two loads
in opposite orders. The SPIR-V emitter numbers a builtin variable when it first sees it, so the
same shader compiles to different SPIR-V on the two architectures: the IDs of the two builtins
swap, they swap places in the OpEntryPoint interface, and the operands of the OpISub swap with
them. The modules are equivalent and never equal, which defeats a cache or a build farm that
compares the bytes.

This emits each load as a statement of its own, in source order. A regression test is added as
tools/slang-unit-test/unit-test-vertex-id-builtin-order.cpp.

Measured on v2026.21, Slang's own release on GitHub-hosted runners: win-x64 and linux-x64 emit
identical SPIR-V and linux-arm64 and osx-arm64 differ from them on exactly the three shaders, of
33, that read SV_VertexID.
